{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 39, :comments ["<b>לא תלך האשה — with me.</b> Rashi z\"l explained that this is why אלי is written defective vav, for the midrash that she was a daughter to Eliezer etc. And it is surprising, for Rabbi Shlomo too explained the verse ״ואם לא תאבה האשה להלך אחריך״ regarding why it does not say my daughter: because Canaanites were already cursed, as it is said ״ארור כנען עבד עבדים״, and if so, why did Abraham not take a wife for his son from the daughters of Anar, Eshcol, and Mamre — for they too were Canaanites. It may be answered that in his case the curse had already been fulfilled, for he was a servant, but they were not servants. And further, it may be said: it is more disdainful to wed one's son to the daughter of his servant. And further, we say in Bereshit Rabbah: Eliezer was the son of Canaan; son of Ham. And some explain: a daughter of his own, and לא תאבה is not written — therefore it says אלי, a hint that she was his daughter. And in Pirkei Rabbi Eliezer, Eliezer servant of Abraham says: I am Og king of Bashan. And thus it is stated there: Eliezer was the elder of the house of our father Abraham, and he wrote upon himself עבד עולם, and when he did kindness with Isaac, [Abraham] took him out to freedom forever, and the Holy One, blessed be He, gave him his reward in this world and made him king, and he is Og king of Bashan. And this is difficult, for Og was wicked and Moses killed him, while Eliezer was righteous — and also in Masekhet Soferim he is counted among the nine who entered the Garden of Eden alive, and on account of this matter he entered: for when he brought Rebecca and Isaac did not find her to be a virgin, as I will explain with Heaven's help, he [Isaac] suspected him [Eliezer] of having had relations with her, and he [Isaac] said to him: ״Thus may you live and enter the Garden of Eden, just as you are innocent of her.״ And that blessing was fulfilled for him, and he entered the Garden of Eden alive. And one must say that there were two Og kings of Bashan: one wicked, whom Moses killed; and one who was a servant and a king, and when he became king his name was called Og, for all kings of Bashan were called Og, just as we find that all kings of Egypt were called Pharaoh."], :book :genesis, :voice :daat-zkenim, :lang :en, :chapter 24, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:06:05.513780193Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}